#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161808 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "232a0173-d743-5696-a6d9-c8279514bcba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8413" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e56fbd2ced76bc02d7c9b29a81f3afab000053d11ec878040f26cef6cc5f65ce.svg)\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$\n![](images/ba/ba1b9ecff7b4825d7eb4fa25ad706c14d04a82b8daa45e524d556c90d71700ca.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161809 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "ad5570e6-4810-5b07-a676-711f0fd069a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8414" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0cb7d70ea0921d1e2e8f2f0a9d8bc06427f29e28b8ed6ea79b5db4ba132f1e0.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_i$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161810 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "d861ebc5-8e1e-5cf0-9b82-5a64ff80cf96" :question/math-academy-id "e-8415" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb8af25c129aac1ef9268d108c073e4b895f5e154c26ddb5692fd527d4eaddc3.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=25.$ Computing $A^T \\! A - 25 I,$ we get\n\n![](images/8c/8c5b88c719ee0e5495e9b0df2c9f4add97a668e74249680fbf378c7c775d92f2.svg)\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T\\!A-25I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/b3/b36812322e6f9128de625a8a2d4e7faf62ba98fb007224c819e2a05738cd7f98.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we get \n![](images/16/1608541af7edcbbb06504c97b907dc2c40a97d9a7c9575d5145c67785f9eb56c.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|v_{11}|+|v_{21}| = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| = \\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161811 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "828e62a9-0076-5ff8-8220-4f779d48eb65" :question/math-academy-id "e-8416" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb6e660e7dd1c933fd2d5c6b6a05fbdf1fe7f5f8ed24faf15f88ea16d7d04889.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-72 & -24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/74/748df21fe8314e02f5a5253edaa39427c38600cd60099ccdfed075a28499d23e.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/08/08573d10464e356dc2eeb7a3a0a4c5f9255d0f96b14da9d63b718b766c1cc8d8.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/252867b0bccd95be918f05320ee65c7153f499b15ccc77624be18128d0f0de09.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9cd8ec-34b0-5264-9342-7001d366e227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369785} {:answer/id #uuid "99a44a66-ca0e-59ed-b288-489aa0bc0e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369786} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce5c0ce-eb03-59b7-acd9-b1e758b553ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369787} {:answer/id #uuid "07fd189d-6d8e-58c7-a251-35f39c4b571d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369788} {:answer/id #uuid "6b293b03-8103-5a42-af9e-675bdac3e64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07fd189d-6d8e-58c7-a251-35f39c4b571d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369788} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a9c185-8587-5c2b-99f9-ccc53cebb3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3faf95ec-a55f-54ec-912a-16a996122518" :question/math-academy-id "q-140632" :question/problem "$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dea2437-4ccf-502f-90d7-8d9c2ee56bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369806} {:answer/id #uuid "6085e643-d1c0-5b27-a31d-c8f17d3991e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369807} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3669b4-5040-571b-913c-4c7797a98323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369808} {:answer/id #uuid "71d09b33-1adc-5eee-9aef-23379e505ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369809} {:answer/id #uuid "a5568416-3171-57fa-8cbb-2d40742e6ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5568416-3171-57fa-8cbb-2d40742e6ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369810} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d0a2152-fc69-533b-8f54-f2ee0661598f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7363044f-37d6-50e2-bea5-62283d710c77" :question/math-academy-id "q-140642" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=18$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{28}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{21}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6055e84-6449-5143-a2b3-46eff0358050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388780} {:answer/id #uuid "1f939b74-aaf7-502f-8564-0b661fd0e6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388781} {:answer/id #uuid "02624a8f-1b6a-5107-a65b-691283905860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388782} {:answer/id #uuid "a8527eb6-3fa6-5558-a401-122a4ccf2932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388783} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ff1e-f85f-5965-ad45-394bcfc443a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02624a8f-1b6a-5107-a65b-691283905860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388782} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7af0c55-c0c0-57b2-8764-cee03bfd3605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "869bbc7e-c91d-54b2-899a-a9769b330e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-140643" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=11$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1865705-beff-552d-ab0f-af824d218256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161295} {:answer/id #uuid "c4996ec0-84d9-563f-b76c-68d6ec28ab8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161296} {:answer/id #uuid "ca34c040-fe8f-5c24-a26d-27b6204592aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161297} {:answer/id #uuid "3daa99c5-9dbb-50f9-ac4d-a415fe493c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161298} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb0842f-4ed9-50b7-9dd7-803b7d0599d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161299}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a9bb34d7-2045-5c65-be09-542822add765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a232bb51-753e-5609-8990-4c7273bd90ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371278} {:answer/id #uuid "50bcbb34-3e49-58da-b9d6-d1f962d92839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371279} {:answer/id #uuid "9957b3c9-6ffe-56d3-b43f-9f8524cc2fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371280} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb422fa-4fbe-5263-bc24-98f5490d8681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371281} {:answer/id #uuid "90b2282a-4067-5201-b7aa-136b26592b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b2282a-4067-5201-b7aa-136b26592b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371282} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab26f280-1837-562a-bb1d-24da664c5ba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d63eea3-e985-5425-b895-8bd787028cdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95735" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8816896f-cca6-5ce2-bf30-8d2b1f66e3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371292} {:answer/id #uuid "2e10c5b6-c997-5994-9093-7e45e10efbd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371293} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fea695-072e-5f95-902a-71d988e363cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371294} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd9c1a8-ef05-5de9-af7d-a1baeb76d255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371295} {:answer/id #uuid "5b42e903-5007-58dd-9a44-08bf6ca76aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8816896f-cca6-5ce2-bf30-8d2b1f66e3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371292} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aac91f8-948d-551f-bd8b-56bc67976e79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa5f61d9-1d7f-5da9-9527-e7cc3f414c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-95904" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=100.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 & 42 \\\\ 42 & 58\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-100I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-100I=[\\begin{aligned}58 & 42 \\\\ 42 & 58\\end{aligned}]-100[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-42 & 42 \\\\ 42 & -42\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "761c0855-151d-5c28-a33c-0a024467af2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385861} {:answer/id #uuid "54dd6618-49c1-5345-aa31-f35e307bf495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385862} {:answer/id #uuid "f649cc86-0ff8-5df8-bae6-aa464f130472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385863} {:answer/id #uuid "e5504555-3d0f-535c-b1c6-d95fb2d03bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385864} {:answer/id #uuid "53e04011-d369-5c0e-8319-75cfcd898bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "761c0855-151d-5c28-a33c-0a024467af2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385861} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ba4b1a-699c-5984-810a-3a206a0122be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd1fb855-b7cc-5891-8c39-1b5365c25012" :question/math-academy-id "q-95905" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332ee583-e727-5bba-8acd-922d23695b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395088} {:answer/id #uuid "a10add95-adc6-5235-9f97-1b43eca22a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395089} {:answer/id #uuid "cd26ab61-b779-5a11-875e-496dff8002aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395090} {:answer/id #uuid "193f3747-9a2d-5cbb-a6bc-941942f42e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395091} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4b6724-9b4a-518a-bc3a-5a3c01258664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193f3747-9a2d-5cbb-a6bc-941942f42e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395091} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d28a695-b25d-5276-8883-5cca1cebedbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5db18361-738b-5a9a-b3b0-1db51bcde1bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95911" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=100,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-100I & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]-100[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 30 \\\\ 30 & -60\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}60 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e43e58b-e171-5bde-8e66-fa1047e3a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404838} {:answer/id #uuid "ad42d289-8707-56c1-bf83-45db2fe36ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404839} {:answer/id #uuid "999d413b-60b1-5bd7-abe7-54bb8a4e91b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404840} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe630aa-18c2-5c17-a5c7-5d9c6cf67fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404841} {:answer/id #uuid "7758da6c-c911-54cb-96cc-7117ea4fabca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad42d289-8707-56c1-bf83-45db2fe36ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404839} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca27652a-1905-5111-863a-956b813d6f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "069d96cb-6a82-58f7-9653-ba67b55165b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95912" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ab0437-c045-5c7f-b38c-be5cb1712a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399297} {:answer/id #uuid "907e5e7f-7e94-57d6-9bfb-14bd7f6ece40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399298} {:answer/id #uuid "0e159865-11e2-5161-bb1a-c643efc6678d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399299} {:answer/id #uuid "57e76023-479d-5f84-af88-1fb8fed76692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399300} {:answer/id #uuid "2546f306-2d7a-5ce6-8172-c0754a16f6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "907e5e7f-7e94-57d6-9bfb-14bd7f6ece40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399298} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b34650b-cc77-5f2b-8326-4e505803a14c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eab879a0-3375-58dd-903d-5ef4248752b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96231" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55eda9fc-807f-5348-8813-d854a5b33209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418103} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb3ccbb-6f9a-5137-a3b4-d0a87f0e7267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418109} {:answer/id #uuid "781d2783-b360-5c7a-a6be-21fa965bb5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418113} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3ebc96-2def-5db3-af96-e1b883783089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 